{"answer":"No. Once the omitted parity justification is supplied, the argument does not prove that √2 is rational: it derives a contradiction from the temporary lowest-terms rational representation. Therefore √2 is irrational. As written, the jump from a² = 2b² to “a and b are both even” needs justification; the parity lemma supplies it.","approaches":[{"description":"Assume a lowest-terms rational representation, use the parity lemma twice, and contradict lowest terms.","id":"parity-reductio","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This directly assesses the stated proof and establishes the opposite conclusion."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary reductio assumption corresponding to the proposed lowest-terms representation.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"id":"lowest-terms-representation","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["square-equation","a-is-even","substitute-a","b-is-even","lowest-terms-contradiction"],"statement":"Assume temporarily that √2 = a/b for integers a,b with b ≠ 0, and that a/b is in lowest terms.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by squaring the assumed equality.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-representation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"square-equation-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a² = 2b².","step_ids":["square-equation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Parity lemma: for an integer n, if n² is even, then n is even; applied to integer a at step a-is-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["square-equation-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"a-even-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a is even.","step_ids":["a-is-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Substitution of a = 2c into a² = 2b² followed by cancellation of 2.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["square-equation-claim","a-even-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-square-even-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, b² is even.","step_ids":["substitute-a"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Parity lemma: for an integer n, if n² is even, then n is even; applied to integer b at step b-is-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["b-square-even-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-even-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, b is even.","step_ids":["b-is-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Both numerator and denominator being even contradicts the lowest-terms condition in the temporary assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["lowest-terms-representation","a-even-claim","b-even-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"contradiction-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The temporary lowest-terms representation yields a contradiction.","step_ids":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Reductio: the temporary rational representation is impossible because it leads to the contradiction at lowest-terms-contradiction.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["contradiction-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"sqrt2-irrational","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"√2 is irrational.","step_ids":["irrational-conclusion"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"square-equation","justification":"Squaring √2 = a/b gives 2 = a²/b²; multiplying by b² gives a² = 2b².","statement":"Under the temporary assumption, obtain a² = 2b²."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation"],"id":"a-is-even","justification":"The right-hand side 2b² is even, so a² is even. The parity lemma applies because a is an integer, hence a is even.","statement":"Conclude that a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation","a-is-even"],"id":"substitute-a","justification":"Write a = 2c. Then 4c² = 2b², so b² = 2c² after cancellation; thus b² is even.","statement":"Derive that b² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["substitute-a"],"id":"b-is-even","justification":"The parity lemma applies because b is an integer and b² is even.","statement":"Conclude that b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["a-is-even","b-is-even"],"id":"lowest-terms-contradiction","justification":"If both a and b are even, they have common factor 2, contrary to the assumption that a/b is in lowest terms.","statement":"Reach a contradiction with the temporary assumption."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["lowest-terms-contradiction"],"id":"irrational-conclusion","justification":"Since the assumed lowest-terms rational representation leads to a contradiction, no such representation exists.","statement":"Conclude that √2 is irrational."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
